    \item $|C \cap P \cap Ch| = 15$
    \item $|C \cap P' \cap Ch'| = 15$ (doesn't take calculus, physics, or chemistry)
    \item $|C| = 180$
    \item $|Ch| = 2 \cdot |P| $
    \item $|C \cap Ch| = 75$
    \item $|P \cap Ch| = 75$
    \item $|P \cap C| = 30$
\end{itemize}

We want to find $|P|$.

Since 15 students take calculus, physics, and chemistry, we have $|C \cap P \cap Ch| = 15$.
Since 15 students don't take any of these, we have $|(C \cup P \cup Ch)'| = 15$.
Thus, $|C \cup P \cup Ch| = 360 - 15 = 345$.

Using the Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion,
$|C \cup P \cup Ch| = |C| + |P| + |Ch| - |C \cap P| - |C \cap Ch| - |P \cap Ch| + |C \cap P \cap Ch|$
$345 = 180 + |P| + |Ch| - 30 - 75 - 75 + 15$
$345 = 180 + |P| + |Ch| - 180 + 15$
$345 = |P| + |Ch| + 15$
$|P| + |Ch| = 330$

Since $|Ch| = 2 \cdot |P|$, we have $|P| + 2|P| = 330$, so $3|P| = 330$.
Therefore, $|P| = \frac{330}{3} = 110$.

Now let's check:
$|Ch| = 2 \cdot 110 = 220$.
$C \cup P \cup Ch = 180 + 110 + 220 - 30 - 75 - 75 + 15 = 510 - 180 + 15 = 330 + 15 = 345$.
This matches our calculation earlier.

We also have the information that $|C \cap P' \cap Ch'| = 15$.
This means that the number of students who take calculus but not physics and not chemistry is 15.
From $|C \cap P \cap Ch| = 15$, and $|C \cap P' \cap Ch'| = 15$, we have $|C| = |C \cap P \cap Ch| + |C \cap P' \cap Ch'| + |C \cap P \cap Ch'| + |C \cap P' \cap Ch|$.
$180 = 15 + 15 + |C \cap P \cap Ch'| + |C \cap P' \cap Ch|$.
We don't know $|C \cap P \cap Ch'|$ or $|C \cap P' \cap Ch|$.

However, we know $|C| = 180$, $|P \cap Ch| = 75$, $|C \cap Ch| = 75$, $|P \cap C| = 30$, $|C \cap P \cap Ch| = 15$.
Let $x = |P|$. Then $|Ch| = 2x$.
$|C \cup P \cup Ch| = |C| + |P| + |Ch| - |C \cap P| - |C \cap Ch| - |P \cap Ch| + |C \cap P \cap Ch|$
$345 = 180 + x + 2x - 30 - 75 - 75 + 15$
$345 = 180 + 3x - 180 + 15$
$345 = 3x + 15$
$330 = 3x$
$x = 110$

